{"tool": "patent_search", "success": true, "data": {"query": "multi screen display seam compensation image stitching blank gap occlusion correction LED wall", "requested_sources": ["arxiv"], "sources": ["arxiv"], "actual_sources_used": ["arxiv"], "source_result_counts": {"arxiv": 10}, "source_status": {"arxiv": {"success": true, "count": 10, "error": null}}, "unavailable_or_empty_sources": [], "skipped_sources": [], "empty_sources": [], "unavailable_reasons": {}, "search_results": [{"reference_id": "1805.09578v1", "title": "Coarse-to-fine Seam Estimation for Image Stitching", "publication_date": null, "applicant": null, "abstract": "Seam-cutting and seam-driven techniques have been proven effective for handling imperfect image series in image stitching. Generally, seam-driven is to utilize seam-cutting to find a best seam from one or finite alignment hypotheses based on a predefined seam quality metric. However, the quality metrics in most methods are defined to measure the average performance of the pixels on the seam without considering the relevance and variance among them. This may cause that the seam with the minimal m", "similarity_score": 0.5, "key_claims": [], "technical_differences": [], "url": "http://arxiv.org/abs/1805.09578v1", "source": "arxiv", "patent_id": "1805.09578v1", "relevance_score": 0.5}, {"reference_id": "1712.08469v1", "title": "Wall modeling via function enrichment: extension to detached-eddy simulation", "publication_date": null, "applicant": null, "abstract": "We extend the approach of wall modeling via function enrichment to detached-eddy simulation. The wall model aims at using coarse cells in the near-wall region by modeling the velocity profile in the viscous sublayer and log-layer. However, unlike other wall models, the full Navier-Stokes equations are still discretely fulfilled, including the pressure gradient and convective term. This is achieved by enriching the elements of the high-order discontinuous Galerkin method with the law-of-the-wall.", "similarity_score": 0.5, "key_claims": [], "technical_differences": [], "url": "http://arxiv.org/abs/1712.08469v1", "source": "arxiv", "patent_id": "1712.08469v1", "relevance_score": 0.5}, {"reference_id": "2311.18564v3", "title": "Leveraging Local Patch Alignment to Seam-cutting for Large Parallax Image Stitching", "publication_date": null, "applicant": null, "abstract": "Seam cutting has shown significant effectiveness in the composition phase of image stitching, particularly for scenarios involving parallax. However, conventional implementations typically position seam-cutting as a downstream process contingent upon successful image alignment. This approach inherently assumes the existence of locally aligned regions where visually plausible seams can be established. Current alignment methods frequently fail to satisfy this prerequisite in large parallax scenari", "similarity_score": 0.5, "key_claims": [], "technical_differences": [], "url": "http://arxiv.org/abs/2311.18564v3", "source": "arxiv", "patent_id": "2311.18564v3", "relevance_score": 0.5}, {"reference_id": "2302.05027v2", "title": "Deep Seam Prediction for Image Stitching Based on Selection Consistency Loss", "publication_date": null, "applicant": null, "abstract": "Image stitching is to construct panoramic images with wider field of vision (FOV) from some images captured from different viewing positions. To solve the problem of fusion ghosting in the stitched image, seam-driven methods avoid the misalignment area to fuse images by predicting the best seam. Currently, as standard tools of the OpenCV library, dynamic programming (DP) and GraphCut (GC) are still the only commonly used seam prediction methods despite the fact that they were both proposed two d", "similarity_score": 0.5, "key_claims": [], "technical_differences": [], "url": "http://arxiv.org/abs/2302.05027v2", "source": "arxiv", "patent_id": "2302.05027v2", "relevance_score": 0.5}, {"reference_id": "1610.08205v1", "title": "W